<LegalLayout
  title="Accessibility statement"
  description="How Data Decision Consulting approaches accessible content, navigation, forms and motion."
  path="/accessibility/"
  updated="24 Aug 2026"
>
  <h2>Commitment</h2>
  <p>
    Data Decision Consulting aims for a website that can be understood and operated across keyboard,
    screen reader, zoom and reduced-motion preferences. The design aims to follow WCAG 2.2 Level AA
    where applicable.
  </p>

  <h2>What the site supports</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Semantic landmarks and one main heading per page.</li><li>
      A skip link and keyboard-accessible navigation.
    </li><li>Visible focus indicators and native disclosure elements for FAQs.</li><li>
      Labels, required-state communication and status messages on the contact form.
    </li><li>High-contrast text and non-color evidence labels.</li><li>
      Responsive layouts for phone, tablet and desktop screens.
    </li><li>Reduced-motion support for animated interface elements.</li><li>
      Tables with header cells and horizontal overflow on small screens.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Known limits</h2>
  <p>
    Automated checks cover only part of the experience. Feedback from people using assistive
    technology is welcome and will guide further improvements.
  </p>

  <h2>Reporting a barrier</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:contact@datadecision.consulting">contact@datadecision.consulting</a> with the
    page, browser or assistive technology involved and a description of the barrier.
  </p>

  <h2>Assessment record</h2>
  <p>
    Data Decision Consulting checks semantic structure, keyboard navigation, focus visibility,
    reduced-motion behavior and representative responsive layouts as part of each website release.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
